  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current approach uses a multi-resolution optimization strategy with SLSQP and coordinate descent, but it may be getting stuck in local optima for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The new approach combines three key improvements:\n\n1. Uses convex relaxations with a PROX layer (piecewise optimal relaxation) to handle the nonconvexity\n2. Implements adaptive multi-resolution switching between coarse and fine grids\n3. Incorporates a symmetry-aware optimization technique\n\nThe core idea is to first find a good candidate solution in low dimensions (complexity reduction) and then scale up. PROX layers enable more reliable handling of the [0,1] box constraints and nonlocal overlap terms.\n\nMathematical specification\nDefine variables:\nh_i = function value at sample point i (\u211d)\nh_ = piecewise constant function representing the approximation (\u211d^\u2115)\nn = number of points\n\nObjective function (after relaxation):\nF(h) = (max_k (h * (1 - h_k)) + (1/T) * log(sum(exp(T*(A_k - max_k A_k)))\n\nConstraints:\n- Each component: 0 <= h_i <= 1\n- Integral constraint: \u2211h_i = n/2\n\nFeasibility: handled via projected gradient with dual variables for the integral constraint.\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Setup parameters: time_budget=1300, max_n=1536, n_start_min=128\n2. Validate parent: if initial_h_values provided, use it then coarsen\n3. Initialization:\n   - Create multiple starts: random perturbations, grid-based, and symmetry variants\n4. Optimization loop:\n   - For each cell size from large to small:\n      \u2022 Separate variables and constraints\n      \u2022 Use ADMM for convex relaxation\n      \u2022 COVariance ADjustment (COVA)\n   - Implement PROX layers for box constraints\n   - Use TRUST-Region method for nonconvex terms\n   - Measurements: use function eval costs per sample\n\nSearch choices\n- Parameter sweep for TRUST-Region radii: [0.1, 0.5, 2]\n- Dynamic resolution switching (based on PROX performance)\n- Extendable via add_subgrid() method\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n- Dominant cost: TRUST-Region iterations (\u2248 200 steps)\n- Total evals\u2248 (budget / 0.05 / \u221adim) (avoid hardware assumptions)\n- Estimated 240 function calls -> 120 real evaluations\n- Check NaNs in ADMM steps\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Re-evaluate using np.correlate with mode='full'\n- Project to [0,1]\u00d7\u222b=1 box\n- Handle gradient explosion via clipping",
